    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-joseph-of-bavaria"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-joseph-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="Maximilian I Joseph of Bavaria" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-joseph-of-bavaria">Maximilian I Joseph of Bavaria</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Max_I_Joseph_in_Coronation_Robe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Joseph Karl Stieler, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> was proclaimed on 1 January 1806, when Elector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-joseph-of-bavaria">Maximilian IV Joseph</a>, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">Wittelsbach dynasty</a> that had ruled Bavaria since 1180, took the title King Maximilian I Joseph. His chief minister, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-von-montgelas">Maximilian von Montgelas</a>, had steered Bavaria into alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, who offered it protection against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and territorial gains. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichsdeputationshauptschluss">Imperial Recess of 1803</a> had already given Bavaria the Franconian prince-bishoprics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> (the latter given up again in 1805), other church lands and imperial cities, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-pressburg-1805">Peace of Pressburg</a> in 1805 added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vorarlberg">Vorarlberg</a> and further Swabian territory together with full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. As a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> from 1806, Bavaria absorbed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>, Ansbach, and in 1810 it gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayreuth">Bayreuth</a>, Regensburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innviertel">Innviertel</a>, bringing it to its greatest extent. In October 1813, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ried">Treaty of Ried</a>, Maximilian changed sides on condition that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> guaranteed his sovereignty. The final settlement came with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-munich-1816">Treaty of Munich</a> of 1816: Bavaria, which had already given back Tyrol and Vorarlberg and gained Würzburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aschaffenburg">Aschaffenburg</a> in 1814, returned Salzburg and the Innviertel to Austria and received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatinate-region">Palatinate</a> on the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. The result was a kingdom of about 76,000 square kilometres, the second largest German state, joining Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-bavaria">Old Bavaria</a> with largely Protestant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> and the Palatinate.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/augsburg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/augsburg.jpg" alt="Augsburg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Augsburg_-_Markt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Guido Radig, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> remained predominantly agrarian throughout the nineteenth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-bavaria">Old Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia-bavaria">Swabia</a> were lands of large peasant farms, Catholic piety and village life, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatinate-region">Palatinate</a> had a stronger Protestant presence, more crafts and a tradition of commerce. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-von-montgelas">Montgelas</a> reforms removed many feudal and church privileges, and the secularisation of 1802–1803 dissolved the monasteries and transferred their estates and libraries to the state. Peasant obligations were finally commuted after 1848. Industry developed in a few centres. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> became a textile and engineering town, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> built machines, toys and electrical equipment, and the firms that merged in 1908 as MAN supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-diesel">Rudolf Diesel</a>'s development of his engine at Augsburg in the 1890s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s first railway, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-railway">Ludwig Railway</a> between Nuremberg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/furth">Fürth</a>, opened in December 1835, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-canal">Ludwig Canal</a> linking the Main and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> was completed in 1846. Brewing was a major industry, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>'s breweries pioneered refrigerated lager production. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oktoberfest">Oktoberfest</a> began with the wedding celebrations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-i-of-bavaria">Crown Prince Ludwig</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/therese-of-saxe-hildburghausen">Therese of Saxe-Hildburghausen</a> in October 1810. Munich grew from about 40,000 inhabitants around 1800 to roughly 600,000 by 1910, when the kingdom had about 6.9 million people. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Bavarians</a>, especially from the Palatinate and Franconia, emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> in the nineteenth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hanau"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-hanau.jpg" alt="Battle of Hanau" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hanau">Battle of Hanau</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vernet-Battle_of_Hanau.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Horace Vernet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Bavarian army fought as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s ally from 1805 to 1813. It took part in the campaigns of 1805, 1806–1807 and 1809, when Bavarian troops fought alongside the French against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and faced the Tyrolean uprising led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andreas-hofer">Andreas Hofer</a>, which they could suppress only with French help. In 1812 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> sent a corps of some 30,000 men into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, where it fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a> and was almost entirely destroyed. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ried">Treaty of Ried</a>, General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-philipp-von-wrede">Karl Philipp von Wrede</a> tried to block Napoleon's retreat but was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hanau">Battle of Hanau</a> in October 1813. Under the German Confederation the Bavarian army formed its own federal army corps. In the war of 1866 Bavaria fought for Austria and its troops were defeated by the Prussians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kissingen">Kissingen</a> in July. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> two Bavarian army corps took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sedan-1870">Battle of Sedan</a>, where Bavarian troops fought the bitter battle for the village of Bazeilles, and in the campaign around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>. Under the treaty by which Bavaria entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>, its army remained under the king's command in peacetime and passed to the emperor only in war. It kept its own war ministry, uniforms and general staff, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupprecht-crown-prince-of-bavaria">Crown Prince Rupprecht of Bavaria</a> commanded a German army and later an army group on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-front-world-war-i">Western Front</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-von-montgelas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maximilian-von-montgelas.jpg" alt="Maximilian von Montgelas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-von-montgelas">Maximilian von Montgelas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maximilian_von_Montgelas_(1806).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Joseph Hauber, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-von-montgelas">Montgelas</a>, chief minister from 1799 to 1817, turned the patchwork of old and new territories into a centralised state on the French model. He created a professional civil service, divided the kingdom into administrative districts named after rivers, introduced religious toleration and compulsory schooling, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> vaccination compulsory in 1807 and commissioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-johann-anselm-von-feuerbach">Anselm von Feuerbach</a>'s criminal code of 1813. After his dismissal in 1817 a concordat with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> was concluded, and the constitution of 26 May 1818 established a parliament of two chambers, a Chamber of Imperial Councillors for princes, nobles and appointed members and an elected Chamber of Deputies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-i-of-bavaria">Ludwig I</a>, king from 1825, began as a liberal but became increasingly authoritarian, especially after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hambach-festival">Hambach Festival</a> of 1832 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatinate-region">Bavarian Palatinate</a>, a mass demonstration for German unity and freedom. His affair with the dancer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lola-montez">Lola Montez</a> discredited him, and he abdicated in March 1848 in favour of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Maximilian II</a>, who accepted liberal reforms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-ii-of-bavaria">Ludwig II</a>, who succeeded in 1864, withdrew increasingly from government, and in June 1886 his ministers had him declared unfit to rule. His uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luitpold-prince-regent-of-bavaria">Luitpold</a> ruled as prince regent for his insane brother Otto until 1912, and Luitpold's son became King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-iii-of-bavaria">Ludwig III</a> in 1913. Within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> kept its own army, railways, posts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraphs</a> and its legations abroad.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/walhalla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walhalla.jpg" alt="Walhalla" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/walhalla">Walhalla</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Walhalla_wie_Parthenon_zu_Ehren_bedeutender_Personen_-_erbaut_1842_-_K%C3%B6nig_Ludwig_I_-_Foto_Wolfgang_Pehlemann_DSCN2430.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wolfgang Pehlemann, CC BY-SA 3.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>'s most lasting legacy is cultural. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-i-of-bavaria">Ludwig I</a> set out to make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> a capital of the arts. His architects <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-von-klenze">Leo von Klenze</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-von-gartner">Friedrich von Gärtner</a> laid out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwigstrasse">Ludwigstrasse</a> and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glyptothek">Glyptothek</a> for classical sculpture and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alte-pinakothek">Alte Pinakothek</a>, opened in 1836, one of the first large public picture galleries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walhalla">Walhalla</a> near Regensburg, completed in 1842, celebrated German national heroes. He moved the university from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a> to Munich in 1826. His son Otto became the first King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in 1832, carrying Bavarian officials and classical taste to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Maximilian II</a> invited scholars from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/historical-commission-bavarian-academy">Historical Commission</a> in 1858 under the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-von-ranke">Leopold von Ranke</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-ii-of-bavaria">Ludwig II</a> made Munich the scene of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-wagner">Richard Wagner</a>'s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tristan-und-isolde">Tristan und Isolde</a></em> in 1865 and helped finance the festival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayreuth">Bayreuth</a>, and his castles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuschwanstein-castle">Neuschwanstein</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linderhof-palace">Linderhof</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herrenchiemsee">Herrenchiemsee</a> became some of the most visited sights in Europe. Around 1900 Munich was a leading art city: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich-secession">Munich Secession</a> was founded in 1892, the magazine <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugend-magazine">Jugend</a></em> gave its name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/art-nouveau">German Art Nouveau</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/der-blaue-reiter">Blue Rider</a> group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wassily-kandinsky">Wassily Kandinsky</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-marc">Franz Marc</a> formed there in 1911. Politically, Bavaria's insistence on its own rights within the empire helped fix the federal character of modern German government.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-world-war.jpg" alt="First World War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_de_Verdun_1916.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Collection DocAnciens/docpix.fr, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> was reduced in two stages. Defeat in 1866 dissolved the German Confederation, cost Bavaria an indemnity and small border districts, and bound it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> by a secret alliance. In 1870 it fought alongside Prussia against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-ii-of-bavaria">Ludwig II</a>, after negotiations in which he received secret payments from Bismarck's funds, signed the so-called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiserbrief">Kaiserbrief</a> inviting William I of Prussia to take the imperial title. The Bavarian parliament approved entry into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> in January 1871 against strong opposition from the Catholic Patriots' Party. Bavaria kept more reserved rights than any other state, but foreign policy, war and most economic legislation were henceforth decided in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. The long regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luitpold-prince-regent-of-bavaria">Luitpold</a> was a period of stability and prosperity, and the Catholic Centre became the dominant party, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> brought heavy casualties, food shortages and growing resentment of Prussian leadership. On 7 November 1918 the Independent Social Democrat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurt-eisner">Kurt Eisner</a> proclaimed a republic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-iii-of-bavaria">Ludwig III</a> fled the capital; on 12 November, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anif-declaration">Anif</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>, he released his officials and soldiers from their oath without formally abdicating. The kingdom became the Free State of Bavaria. After 1945 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatinate-region">Palatinate</a> was separated, and the Free State remains a state of the Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Created as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s ally in 1806 and remodelled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-von-montgelas">Montgelas</a> into a centralised constitutional state, the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> was the second German power after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> a European art capital under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-i-of-bavaria">Ludwig I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-ii-of-bavaria">Ludwig II</a>, and secured the federal character of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>.</em></p>

